.TH TDE\-NMCTL 1 "November 2026" "tde-nmctl 0.1.0" "Trinity Desktop Environment"
.SH NAME
tde\-nmctl \- network connection editor for NetworkManager
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tde\-nmctl
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR page ]
.RB [ \-\-select
.IR name ]
.RI [ "generic TDE and TQt options" ]
.br
.B tde\-nmctl
.BI \-\-edit " connection"
.RB [ \-\-tab
.IR number ]
.br
.B tde\-nmctl
.BI \-\-new " type"
.RB [ \-\-tab
.IR number ]
.br
.B tde\-nmctl
.B \-\-list
|
.BI \-\-show " connection"
.br
.B tdecmshell nmctl
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tde\-nmctl
manages the network connections of
.BR NetworkManager (8)
in the Trinity Desktop Environment. Its window is modelled on
.BR nm\-connection\-editor (1)
and
.BR plasma\-nm .
.PP
The
.B Connections
page lists the connection profiles grouped as wired, Wi\-Fi, VPN, mobile
and virtual, with their state and when they were last used. Profiles can be
added, edited, deleted, duplicated, connected and disconnected; VPN
configurations can be imported from and exported to
.I .ovpn
and WireGuard
.I .conf
files (through
.BR nmcli (1)).
.PP
The editor has a tab for each group of settings: General (name, automatic
connection and its priority, users, VPN started with the connection,
firewall zone, metered), Ethernet, Wi\-Fi, Wi\-Fi Security (WEP, WPA/WPA2/WPA3
Personal and Enterprise, OWE, LEAP), 802.1X (TLS, PEAP, TTLS, PWD, LEAP,
FAST), VPN (the NetworkManager VPN plugins: OpenVPN, vpnc, OpenConnect,
L2TP, PPTP, strongSwan, Libreswan, Fortinet, SSTP, SSH), WireGuard, Mobile
Broadband (GSM/LTE, CDMA), DSL/PPPoE, InfiniBand, Bond, Bridge, Team, VLAN,
IPv4 and IPv6 (method, addresses, DNS, search domains, routes) and Proxy.
.PP
The
.B Devices
page shows every network device with its driver, hardware address, speed,
addresses, DNS servers and traffic, and the Wi\-Fi networks in range.
The
.B Settings
page turns networking, Wi\-Fi and mobile broadband on and off, sets the
connectivity check and the host name, and chooses where new passwords are
stored.
.PP
The same pages are available as a Trinity Control Center module, under
.B Internet & Network \(-> Network Connections
or with
.BR "tdecmshell nmctl" ,
and in System Settings under
.BR "Network & Connectivity \(-> Network Settings" .
The system tray icon, which also hands passwords to NetworkManager, is
.BR tde\-nmctl\-tray (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " page"
The page shown at start:
.BR connections " (default), " devices " or " settings .
.TP
.BI \-\-select " name"
Select the connection with this name or UUID in the list, or with
.B \-\-page devices
the device with this interface name.
.TP
.BI \-\-edit " connection"
Open the editor for the connection with this name or UUID and exit when it
is closed. The exit status is 0 if the connection was saved.
.TP
.BI \-\-new " type"
Open the editor for a new connection and exit when it is closed.
.I type
is one of
.BR ethernet ", " wifi ", " vpn ", " wireguard ", " bond ", " bridge ", " team ", "
.BR vlan ", " gsm ", " cdma ", " pppoe ", " infiniband " or " dummy ,
or a NetworkManager setting name such as
.BR 802\-11\-wireless .
.TP
.BI \-\-tab " number"
With
.B \-\-edit
or
.BR \-\-new :
the tab of the editor shown first, counted from 0.
.TP
.B \-\-list
Print NetworkManager's state, the devices with the Wi\-Fi networks in range
and the connection profiles, then exit. Needs no X display.
.TP
.BI \-\-show " connection"
Print the settings of a connection (without its secrets) and exit.
.SH PASSWORDS
Every password field has a choice of storage, as in nm\-connection\-editor:
.TP
.B Store in TDE Wallet (this user)
The password belongs to the user (NetworkManager's
.I agent\-owned
flag). It is kept in TDE Wallet, in the folder
.B tde\-nmctl
of the network wallet, and handed to NetworkManager by the secret agent in
.BR tde\-nmctl\-tray (1).
This is the default for new passwords.
.TP
.B Store for all users (unencrypted)
NetworkManager stores the password itself, readable by root, in
.IR /etc/NetworkManager/system\-connections .
.TP
.B Ask every time
The password is never stored; the tray asks for it when connecting.
.SH AUTHORIZATION
NetworkManager checks every change with
.BR polkit (8).
Changing connections that all users may use, the host name, or turning
networking off usually needs the administrator's password, which a polkit
authentication agent such as
.B polkit\-agent\-tde
asks for. tde\-nmctl uses polkit\-tqt to show beforehand what will need
authentication and warns if no agent is running. Connections for the
current user only can usually be changed without a password.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.trinity/share/config/tde\-nmctlrc
Settings: password storage, notifications, the list of networks in the
tray.
.TP
.I ~/.trinity/share/config/tde\-nmctl\-trayrc
Whether the tray icon starts at login.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B tde\-nmctl \-\-new wifi
Create a Wi\-Fi connection.
.TP
.B tde\-nmctl \-\-edit """Wired connection 1""" \-\-tab 3
Edit the IPv4 settings of a wired connection.
.TP
.B tde\-nmctl \-\-list
Show what NetworkManager is doing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tde\-nmctl\-tray (1),
.BR NetworkManager (8),
.BR nmcli (1),
.BR nm\-settings (5),
.BR tdecmshell (1)
.PP
